WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:06.810
GOOD: training-free guided diffusion sampling for out-of-
distribution detection.

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:14.556
A NeurIPS 2025 paper from Nanjing University, Fudan and
collaborators.

00:00:15.906 --> 00:00:30.570
Outlier exposure is one of the more reliable ways to improve
OOD detection. Show a classifier some auxiliary out-of-

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:38.604
The catch has always been supply. Hand-curated outlier
datasets are expensive and tied to a domain.

00:00:39.054 --> 00:00:51.150
So recent work generates them with diffusion models. This
paper's argument is that the standard recipe for doing so is

00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:05.988
Methods like Dream-OOD work in two stages. First they map in-
distribution images into the diffusion model's text-

00:01:06.438 --> 00:01:22.686
The first problem is misalignment. A small nudge in embedding
space produces a large and unpredictable shift in pixel space,

00:01:23.136 --> 00:01:39.768
The second is coverage. Perturbing a semantic embedding gives
you semantic shift and nothing else. Covariate shift and

00:01:40.218 --> 00:01:46.482
Both problems come from the same choice: editing a text
embedding rather than the image itself.

00:01:47.832 --> 00:01:51.360
GOOD deletes the embedding stage entirely.

00:01:51.810 --> 00:02:04.026
It starts from a diffusion model conditioned on a plain class
prompt, and steers the denoising trajectory itself using

00:02:04.476 --> 00:02:12.756
The resulting images go into outlier exposure training, and at
test time a unified score combines two signals.

00:02:13.206 --> 00:02:22.758
Nothing is retrained. The classifier is frozen, the diffusion
model is frozen, and there is no latent space to build first.

00:02:24.108 --> 00:02:33.708
Diffusion sampling walks a noisy sample inward across a family
of manifolds, from pure noise toward the data manifold.

00:02:34.158 --> 00:02:40.350
GOOD adds two corrections at each step, following the
Training-Free Guidance framework.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:52.488
Variance guidance takes the gradient with respect to the noisy
sample and carries second-order information. It matters most

00:02:52.938 --> 00:03:03.882
Mean guidance takes the gradient with respect to the denoised
estimate and acts in data space. It matters most late, when

00:03:04.332 --> 00:03:12.732
Both use an increasing schedule, so guidance is weak while
gradients are noisy and strengthens as the image resolves.

00:03:14.082 --> 00:03:19.650
The first guidance signal comes from reading the classifier as
an energy-based model.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:30.924
The free energy is the negative log-sum-exp of the logits. It
is a negative log-density up to a partition function that does

00:03:31.374 --> 00:03:38.934
So low energy means the classifier finds the input typical;
high energy means atypical.

00:03:39.384 --> 00:03:46.680
That is a differentiable function of the pixels, available
from any classifier, with no extra training.

00:03:48.030 --> 00:04:00.246
Because energy is a negative log-density, ascending its
gradient walks a sample out of the high-density region the

00:04:00.696 --> 00:04:11.280
This is a pixel-space signal, so it captures the shifts the
embedding-perturbation methods miss — style, colour, material,

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:22.458
The paper's qualitative results show exactly that: images that
keep the rough class layout but render it with surreal colours

00:04:23.808 --> 00:04:28.344
The second signal works in the classifier's feature space
instead.

00:04:28.794 --> 00:04:38.778
They build a bank of normalised in-distribution feature
vectors, and for any sample compute the distance to its k-th

00:04:39.228 --> 00:04:47.964
Small distance means the sample sits inside a dense, well-
covered region. Large distance means it has rare features.

00:04:48.414 --> 00:05:06.462
Ascending that gradient pushes toward feature-sparse regions,
which produces something quite different from the energy

00:05:06.912 --> 00:05:14.976
Notably, k barely matters — anywhere from 50 to 500 gives
AUROC around 96.2.

00:05:16.326 --> 00:05:24.966
Put together, one sampling step is the ordinary DDIM update
plus the two guidance terms, each scaled by its own step size.

00:05:25.416 --> 00:05:41.136
One detail bridges a gap: the guidance functions are defined
on clean images, but the sampler operates on noisy ones. So

00:05:41.586 --> 00:05:54.522
The paper also drops recurrence and inner iterations, setting
both to one, because outlier exposure needs tens of thousands

00:05:55.872 --> 00:05:59.544
The two step sizes turn out to be the real control knob.

00:05:59.994 --> 00:06:11.826
Sweeping them over a five-by-five grid, the energy
distribution of the generated samples shifts steadily away

00:06:12.276 --> 00:06:27.276
That creates a dilemma. Samples too close to the in-
distribution data are indistinguishable and regularise

00:06:27.726 --> 00:06:35.934
Rather than grid-searching for a single best setting, they
generate across all twenty-five combinations and pool the

00:06:36.384 --> 00:06:47.664
That is where the twelve and a half thousand image-guided
samples and ten and a half thousand feature-guided samples

00:06:49.014 --> 00:06:53.742
The two signals fail the classifier in visibly different ways.

00:06:54.192 --> 00:07:06.912
Image-level guidance distorts globally: the foreground,
background and object boundaries all drift toward unnatural

00:07:07.362 --> 00:07:19.266
Feature-level guidance keeps the image photorealistic and
instead introduces rare class-specific features — a bird in an

00:07:19.716 --> 00:07:31.956
Grad-CAM confirms the mechanism: for the hen class, the
distortions concentrate on the comb, feathers and claws, which

00:07:32.406 --> 00:07:42.006
So the guidance is not adding generic noise. It is finding the
parts of the image that carry the classifier's decision and

00:07:43.356 --> 00:07:48.756
The synthetic outliers then go into ordinary outlier exposure
training.

00:07:49.206 --> 00:07:59.142
A small MLP head is trained on the free energy to separate in-
distribution from synthetic OOD, added to the usual cross-

00:07:59.592 --> 00:08:15.744
Lambda controls the trade. Higher values improve detection and
slowly erode in-distribution accuracy; they settle on 2.5,

00:08:17.094 --> 00:08:22.614
At test time the same two signals are reused, but now the
question is how to weight them.

00:08:23.064 --> 00:08:35.160
The paper's answer is adaptive. Compare the k-NN distance
distribution of the test set against a held-out in-

00:08:35.610 --> 00:08:46.938
A large divergence means the shift is visible in feature
space, so trust the feature distance. A small one means it is

00:08:47.388 --> 00:08:59.532
This matters because the datasets genuinely differ. Textures
separates cleanly in feature space; iNaturalist, Places and

00:08:59.982 --> 00:09:03.702
A fixed weight would win on one group and lose on the other.

00:09:05.052 --> 00:09:08.700
The ablation separates the contributions.

00:09:09.150 --> 00:09:20.646
The plain classifier sits at 48.68 FPR95. Either guidance
alone, with balanced sampling, brings it to roughly 32.

00:09:21.096 --> 00:09:28.200
Using both together reaches 30.82 — a real but modest gain
over either alone.

00:09:28.650 --> 00:09:36.546
And then adding the unified score takes it to 17.70. That is
by far the largest single step.

00:09:36.996 --> 00:09:47.964
Worth stating plainly: much of the headline improvement comes
from the test-time scoring function, not from the generated

00:09:49.314 --> 00:09:59.250
On ImageNet-100, GOOD reaches 17.68 FPR95 and 96.30 AUROC.

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:11.220
That is close to half the false positive rate of NCIS, the
strongest previous synthesis method, and it holds across

00:10:11.670 --> 00:10:19.302
Textures is the exception — GOOD gets 19.51 there, behind NPOS
at 8.98.

00:10:19.752 --> 00:10:35.304
The authors are direct about why: every pixel-level synthesis
method struggles on Textures, because Stable Diffusion was

00:10:36.654 --> 00:10:40.206
On CIFAR-100 something counterintuitive happens.

00:10:40.656 --> 00:10:53.712
Prior methods run a 512-by-512 diffusion model and then
downsample to CIFAR's 32-by-32, which misaligns pixel and

00:10:54.162 --> 00:11:06.042
GOOD's fully differentiable path means it can use a 128-by-128
generator with bilinear downsampling instead, and gradients

00:11:06.492 --> 00:11:23.100
The smaller model wins — 19.50 FPR95 against 25.66 — while
costing an order of magnitude less compute. Only on the highly

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:28.386
The cost argument is structural rather than incremental.

00:11:28.836 --> 00:11:39.300
Dream-OOD, NCIS and BOOD all need to build a latent space,
sample OOD embeddings in it, decode to images, and then train.

00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:56.238
Dream-OOD reports over sixteen hours on ImageNet-100 — eight
for the latent space, ten for generation, eight for training.

00:11:56.688 --> 00:12:01.224
GOOD has two stages: guided generation and training.

00:12:01.674 --> 00:12:17.370
Per image it is about five point seven times slower than
unconditional sampling, because of the classifier's forward

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:30.264
To summarise: the contribution is to move OOD synthesis from
embedding perturbation to direct guidance of the sampler,

00:12:30.714 --> 00:12:43.362
Two complementary signals — free energy for global
abstraction, k-NN sparsity for realistic hard negatives — and

00:12:43.812 --> 00:12:45.684
Three caveats, though.

